    <body>
        <h2>Student Rights & Discipline in District 32 (Bushwick): A Parent's Guide</h2>

        <p>In NYC's District 32 (Bushwick), understanding student rights within the disciplinary process is essential for parents and guardians. This guide focuses on discipline, Manifestation Determination Reviews (MDRs), and due process rights for students, particularly those with disabilities. We will provide actionable advice, legal references, and advocacy tips to help you navigate the system effectively.</p>

        <h3>Understanding Student Discipline in District 32: General Education Students</h3>

        <p>All students in District 32, regardless of disability status, are subject to the NYC Department of Education's Citywide Standards of Discipline and Intervention Measures. This document outlines specific infractions and corresponding disciplinary actions, ranging from verbal warnings to suspension and expulsion (though expulsion is rare). It's crucial to familiarize yourself with this document, which can usually be found on the NYC DOE website or by requesting a copy from your child's school. You have the right to a clear explanation of any alleged infraction and the proposed disciplinary action.</p>

        <p><b>Parent Tip:</b> Always request a written explanation of the charges against your child. This document should include specific details about the incident, the date, time, location, witnesses, and the specific rule your child allegedly violated. This information is crucial for building a defense or understanding the school's perspective.</p>

        <h3>Manifestation Determination Review (MDR): A Critical Safeguard for Students with Disabilities</h3>

        <p>A Manifestation Determination Review (MDR) is a legally mandated process under the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) 20 U.S.C. § 1415(k) and New York State Education Law Part 200. This review is triggered when a student with an Individualized Education Program (IEP) or a Section 504 plan faces a disciplinary action that involves a suspension exceeding 10 consecutive school days or a pattern of removals that constitutes a change of placement. The purpose of the MDR is to determine whether the student's misconduct was caused by, or directly and substantially related to, their disability, or whether the misconduct was a direct result of the school's failure to implement the student's IEP or 504 plan.  If either condition is met, the behavior is considered a "manifestation" of the disability.</p>

        <p><b>The MDR Team:</b> The MDR team typically includes the parent(s)/guardian(s), the student (when appropriate), a representative of the school district (often a school psychologist, special education teacher, or administrator), and relevant members of the student's IEP team. It's critical to ensure all relevant parties are present and that their perspectives are considered.</p>

        <h4>The MDR Process: A Step-by-Step Guide</h4>

        <ol>
            <li><b>Notification:</b> The school must notify you of the disciplinary action and your right to an MDR within a specific timeframe (usually within 10 school days of the decision to change the student's placement due to a disciplinary infraction). Document the date you received this notification.</li>
            <li><b>Record Review:</b> The MDR team will review all relevant information in the student's file, including the IEP or 504 plan, any psychological or educational evaluations, teacher observations, and any relevant medical information. As a parent, you should provide any information you believe is relevant, such as outside evaluations or medical diagnoses.</li>
            <li><b>Discussion and Determination:</b> The team will discuss the student's behavior and determine whether it was a manifestation of their disability or a result of the school's failure to implement the IEP/504 plan. This determination must be made based on a preponderance of the evidence.</li>
            <li><b>Documentation:</b> The outcome of the MDR must be documented in writing, including the reasons for the determination. You are entitled to a copy of this documentation.</li>
        </ol>

        <h4>What Happens if the Behavior is a Manifestation of the Disability?</h4>

        <p>If the MDR team determines that the student's behavior was a manifestation of their disability or a result of the school's failure to implement the IEP/504 plan, the school cannot impose a suspension exceeding 10 days or any other disciplinary action that constitutes a change of placement. Instead, the school must take immediate steps to address the student's needs and prevent future incidents. This may include:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><b>Revising the IEP or 504 Plan:</b> The IEP team must meet to review and revise the IEP or 504 plan to address the student's behavioral needs. This may involve adding or modifying behavioral goals, providing additional supports and services, or changing the student's placement.</li>
            <li><b>Conducting a Functional Behavioral Assessment (FBA):</b> If one has not already been conducted, the school must conduct an FBA to identify the triggers and functions of the student's behavior.</li>
            <li><b>Developing a Behavior Intervention Plan (BIP):</b> Based on the results of the FBA, the school must develop a BIP to teach the student appropriate replacement behaviors and provide positive reinforcement for those behaviors.</li>
            <li><b>Returning the Student to Their Original Placement:</b> Unless the parents and the school agree to a change of placement as part of the IEP revision process, the student must be returned to their original placement.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>What Happens if the Behavior is NOT a Manifestation of the Disability?</h4>

        <p>If the MDR team determines that the student's behavior was not a manifestation of their disability and that the school properly implemented the IEP/504 plan, the student can be disciplined in the same manner as a student without a disability. However, the school must still provide the student with a Free Appropriate Public Education (FAPE), even during the period of suspension or expulsion. This may involve providing alternative educational services, such as tutoring or home instruction.</p>

        <p><b>Important Note:</b> Even if the behavior is not a manifestation, the school must consider the student's disability when determining the appropriate disciplinary action. For example, a student with ADHD may have difficulty controlling impulsive behaviors, and the school should take this into account when deciding on a consequence.</p>

        <h3>Advice for Suspensions in District 32: A Parent Advocacy Guide</h3>

        <p>Navigating the suspension process can be daunting. Here's a breakdown of actionable steps you can take to protect your child's rights:</p>

        <ol>
            <li><b>Immediate Action:</b> As soon as you are notified of a potential suspension, request a written explanation of the charges, including the specific rule(s) violated, date, time, location, and witnesses.</li>
            <li><b>Documentation is Key:</b> Keep detailed records of all communication with the school, including dates, times, who you spoke with, and the content of the conversation. Save all emails and letters.</li>
            <li><b>Attend All Meetings:</b> Actively participate in all meetings related to the suspension. Ask questions, present your child's perspective, and provide any relevant information. Bring a support person or advocate with you if possible.</li>
            <li><b>Understand Your Child's Rights:</b> Familiarize yourself with the NYC DOE's Citywide Standards of Discipline and Intervention Measures and your child's rights under IDEA and Section 504 (if applicable).</li>
            <li><b>Request an MDR (If Applicable):</b> If your child has an IEP or 504 plan and the suspension is for more than 10 days (or constitutes a pattern of removals), immediately request an MDR in writing.</li>
            <li><b>Challenge the Suspension:</b> You have the right to appeal a suspension decision. The process for appealing a suspension is outlined in the NYC DOE's Citywide Standards of Discipline and Intervention Measures. Make sure you adhere to the timelines for filing an appeal.</li>
            <li><b>Consider Legal Counsel or Advocacy Support:</b> If you believe your child's rights have been violated, the disciplinary action is unfair, or you need assistance navigating the process, seek legal counsel from a special education attorney or contact a parent advocacy organization.</li>
        </ol>

        <h4>Common District 32 Pitfalls to Watch Out For:</h4>

        <ul>
            <li><b>Failure to Conduct Timely MDRs:</b> Some schools may delay or fail to conduct MDRs within the required timeframe. This is a violation of IDEA.</li>
            <li><b>Inadequate IEP Implementation:</b> Disciplinary actions may arise because the school is not properly implementing the student's IEP. Document any instances of non-compliance.</li>
            <li><b>Lack of FBA/BIP:</b> Students with behavioral challenges should have an FBA and BIP in place. If your child does not have these, request them in writing.</li>
            <li><b>Disproportionate Discipline:</b> Students with disabilities, and particularly students of color with disabilities, are often disproportionately disciplined. Be aware of this potential bias and advocate for fair treatment.</li>
            <li><b>Informal Suspensions:</b> Schools may try to avoid formal suspension procedures by sending a student home for a "cooling off" period. These informal suspensions still count towards the 10-day threshold for triggering an MDR and should be documented.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Due Process Rights in District 32: Protecting Your Child's Education</h3>

        <p>If you disagree with the school's decisions regarding your child's special education services or disciplinary actions, you have the right to due process under IDEA. This includes the right to:</p>

        <ul>
            <li><b>Mediation:</b> A voluntary process where you and the school district attempt to resolve your dispute with the help of a neutral mediator.</li>
            <li><b>Impartial Hearing:</b> A formal hearing before an impartial hearing officer (IHO) who will make a decision based on the evidence presented. You have the right to present evidence, call witnesses, and cross-examine witnesses.</li>
            <li><b>Appeal:</b> If you disagree with the IHO's decision, you have the right to appeal to the State Review Officer (SRO).</li>
        </ul>

        <p><b>Filing for Due Process:</b> To initiate due process, you must file a written complaint with the New York State Education Department (NYSED). The complaint must include specific information about the nature of the dispute and the relief you are seeking.</p>

        <p><b>Statute of Limitations:</b> There is a statute of limitations for filing a due process complaint, so it is important to act quickly. In New York State, you generally have two years from the date you knew or should have known about the alleged violation to file a complaint. [See 8 NYCRR §200.5(j)]</p>

        <h3>FAQ: Discipline and Special Education in District 32</h3>

        <dl>
            <dt><b>Q: What is a "change of placement" in the context of discipline?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: A change of placement occurs when a student is removed from their current educational setting for more than 10 consecutive school days or when a series of removals creates a pattern that amounts to a change in educational placement. This determination is often fact-specific and considers factors like the length of each removal, the proximity of the removals to each other, and the total amount of time the student is excluded from school.  A change of placement triggers the right to an MDR.</dd>

            <dt><b>Q: My child has ADHD but doesn't have an IEP. Are they still protected under IDEA during disciplinary actions?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: If your child has ADHD and you suspect it impacts their behavior, you should request an evaluation for special education services. While they are not automatically protected under IDEA without an IEP or 504 plan, they may be protected under Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act if their ADHD substantially limits a major life activity (e.g., learning). Request a 504 evaluation immediately. If a school "knew or should have known" that a student had a disability prior to the behavior that led to disciplinary action, even without a formal IEP or 504 plan, the student may still have some protections under IDEA. This is known as the "child find" obligation.</dd>

            <dt><b>Q: Can a school suspend a student with an IEP for behavior that occurs off-campus?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: Yes, a school can suspend a student with an IEP for behavior that occurs off-campus if the behavior is substantially related to the school environment or poses a threat to the safety of students or staff. However, the school must still conduct an MDR if the suspension exceeds 10 days or constitutes a change of placement.</dd>

            <dt><b>Q: What should I do if I disagree with the results of the MDR?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: If you disagree with the results of the MDR, you have the right to challenge the decision through due process. File a due process complaint with NYSED and consider seeking legal counsel from a special education attorney.</dd>

            <dt><b>Q: My child is being bullied. Can this affect disciplinary actions they might face?</b></dt>
            <dd>A: Yes. If your child's behavior is a direct result of being bullied, that needs to be brought to the attention of the school and considered during any disciplinary proceedings. Bullying can be a significant contributing factor, and the school has a responsibility to address the bullying situation as well. This can be a strong argument during an MDR. Document all instances of bullying and report them to the school in writing.</dd>
        </dl>

    </body>
